    {
      "text": "Every offering to the fire god Agni ends with the word Swaha. The gods once starved while humans fed the fire regularly. The fire god himself said it was his fault. One morning, the gods came to Brahma, the creator, in Brahmaloka. They were thin and weak. They had nothing to eat. Brahma explained how they were meant to eat. When humans held a yajna, a fire ritual, they poured offerings into the fire. Agni, the mouth of the gods, carried them to heaven. In return, the gods gave humans blessings. Indra, a leader of the gods, said humans held yajnas all the time, but nothing reached heaven. Another god, Kamadev, blamed Agni. Agni stepped forward with folded hands. He said he was to blame. He could burn the offerings, but he could not carry them to the gods. He did not have that skill. Brahma had no answer. Vishnu, the preserver, told him to pray to Prakriti, mother nature. Prakriti created a young goddess to carry offerings from earth to heaven. Brahma asked her name. \"Swaha,\" she said. Brahma declared that every offering to Agni must end with her name. Without it, the offering would not reach the gods. A chant without Swaha would be like a tree without fruit, a snake without venom, a man without knowledge. Then he gave Swaha in marriage to Agni, and the two were never parted.",
      "start_time": 0.16,
      "end_time": 82.62,
